== explicit unsupported platform policy ==
# Installing Boreal Work v2

The supported release artifact is a platform-specific archive containing the
`bwrk` executable, compiled TUI files, release identity, and license. Project
databases are not part of the install and are never created as an install side
effect. Installation automatically provisions a separate per-user global
manager SQLite database, including CLI-only installations. The global manager
is independent of the installation prefix and any project folder. Source
builds are supported on Apple Silicon and Intel macOS and x86_64 Linux.
The release workflow builds candidate archives for macOS ARM64, macOS x86-64,
and Linux x86-64. These workflow artifacts are qualification inputs; they are
not published releases or platform-support claims. Published v0.2.1 archives
cover Apple Silicon macOS and x86_64 Linux. Linux ARM64 and Windows remain
unsupported until their transport and SQLite runtime builds are verified.

The CLI works without Node.js. The project and Global dashboards require
Node.js 20 through 26 and report an actionable error before starting their
private service when that runtime is unavailable or outside the supported
range. Boreal checks the selected executable's version before starting the
service or TUI. Set `BOREAL_NODE` to select the project dashboard runtime or
`BOREAL_NODE_BINARY` for the Global dashboard; each can be an executable path
or a command name. JSON snapshots remain usable without Node.js.

## Install or update from GitHub

For Apple Silicon macOS and x86_64 Linux, use the prebuilt release installer. It fetches the installer from `main`, selects the compatible archive from the latest GitHub release, verifies the archive against `SHA256SUMS`, and installs it under `~/.local`. The current release, v0.2.1, includes those two platform archives. The release workflow retains candidate packages as workflow artifacts and does not publish GitHub Releases. The install process does not invoke GitHub Actions or modify project databases.

```sh
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mattrichmo/boreal-work/refs/heads/main/install.sh | sh -s -- --yes
bwrk --version
```

To pin the currently published version, pass `--version 0.2.1`:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mattrichmo/boreal-work/refs/heads/main/install.sh | sh -s -- --version 0.2.1 --yes
```

### Source-build fallback

`build_release.py` adds those generated assets, records Rust/Node/TypeScript
toolchain versions, and creates the archive. The current process still does
not sign artifacts, contact a registry, pause active attempts during an
upgrade, or migrate a project database. The direct installer runs Global
bootstrap after publishing package files and can restore the previous package
on failure, but it does not yet pair package rollback with restoration of a
matching physical Global database backup. Keep a verified Global backup for
updates that may require a Global schema migration. No checked-in workflow
publishes release archives or a rendered Homebrew formula. The formula can be
rendered locally from reviewed archives with
`scripts/release/render_homebrew_formula.py`; publishing it to a tap is a
separate manual release step. These remaining behaviors are release-gate work.

The direct installer downloads `SHA256SUMS` from the same GitHub Release as
the selected archive and verifies the archive digest. This detects transfer or
artifact mismatches but does not authenticate the release with an independent
signature. The `.github/workflows/release.yml` workflow creates retained
qualification artifacts; it does not publish them as GitHub Releases.

The identity simulation is deliberately not a live installer: its target and
rollback backup are synthetic directories inside a caller-created temp
directory. `install.sh` is the real archive installer and performs a bounded,
backup-preserving replacement under the selected prefix. Active-attempt
compatibility checks, signing, and full platform-specific upgrade/recovery
tests remain release-gate work.

